((window.CARDS_BY_LANG = window.CARDS_BY_LANG || {}).en = window.CARDS_BY_LANG.en || {}).club = [{"name":"Real Madrid","emoji":"👑","meta":["football","Madrid, Spain","1902"],"description":"The Madrid club whose name simply means \"royal\" plays in all white and built its reputation on signing the biggest stars of every era, from Di Stéfano to Ronaldo. Home is the legendary Santiago Bernabéu stadium.","fact":"Real Madrid has won more UEFA Champions League titles than any other club in Europe.","wiki_en":"Real Madrid CF"},{"name":"Barcelona","emoji":"🔵","meta":["football","Barcelona, Spain","1899"],"description":"The club from the Catalan capital plays in blue and claret and has long been the region's calling card — its motto \"More than a club\" speaks to the bond with Catalan identity. Home is Camp Nou, one of the largest stadiums in Europe.","fact":"The club's La Masia academy produced a long line of world stars, Lionel Messi among them.","wiki_en":"FC Barcelona"},{"name":"Atlético Madrid","emoji":"🐻","meta":["football","Madrid, Spain","1903"],"description":"The Spanish capital's other big club plays in red and white stripes and is known for stubborn, disciplined football, above all under coach Diego Simeone. Fans nicknamed the team Los Colchoneros — \"the mattress makers\" — after those striped shirts.","fact":"The club's crest shows a bear reaching up to a strawberry tree, the emblem taken from Madrid's coat of arms.","wiki_en":"Atlético Madrid"},{"name":"Manchester United","emoji":"😈","meta":["football","Manchester, England","1878"],"description":"One of the most widely supported football clubs in the world, playing in red and known as the Red Devils. Its history runs from the tragedy of the 1958 Munich air disaster to the golden era under Alex Ferguson.","fact":"The club's home ground, Old Trafford, is known as the Theatre of Dreams.","wiki_en":"Manchester United F.C."},{"name":"Manchester City","emoji":"🩵","meta":["football","Manchester, England","1880"],"description":"The sky-blue half of Manchester, transformed after a takeover in the late 2000s into one of the most successful sides in England and in Europe. Home is the Etihad Stadium, on the east side of the city.","fact":"Fans and rivals alike call the club simply the Citizens.","wiki_en":"Manchester City F.C."},{"name":"Liverpool","emoji":"🐦","meta":["football","Liverpool, England","1892"],"description":"A club with one of the richest records in European competition, whose anthem \"You'll Never Walk Alone\" is sung by the whole ground before every home match. Liverpool play in red at Anfield, their home since the club was founded.","fact":"The club's crest carries the liver bird, the legendary symbol of the city.","wiki_en":"Liverpool F.C."},{"name":"Chelsea","emoji":"🦁","meta":["football","London, England","1905"],"description":"The London club in blue rose sharply in English and European football after a Russian owner took over in the early 2000s and poured money into the squad. Home is Stamford Bridge, a compact ground hemmed in by the streets of west London.","fact":"Supporters call the team simply the Blues.","wiki_en":"Chelsea F.C."},{"name":"Arsenal","emoji":"💥","meta":["football","London, England","1886"],"description":"One of London's oldest football clubs, nicknamed the Gunners after the armaments works where it was founded. It is known for attacking, technical football, above all during Arsène Wenger's years in charge.","fact":"Under Wenger the club went a whole league season unbeaten — a feat almost unheard of in English football.","wiki_en":"Arsenal F.C."},{"name":"Tottenham Hotspur","emoji":"🐓","meta":["football","London, England","1882"],"description":"The north London club with a cockerel on its badge, known for attacking football and for a fierce rivalry with Arsenal in the North London derby. It plays at the modern Tottenham Hotspur Stadium.","fact":"The Hotspur in its name honours the medieval knight Harry Percy, nicknamed Hotspur, whose family held land in this part of London.","wiki_en":"Tottenham Hotspur F.C."},{"name":"Bayern Munich","emoji":"🏆","meta":["football","Munich, Germany","1900"],"description":"Germany's most decorated club has dominated the national league for decades and is a regular contender for the Champions League. Bayern play in red at the Allianz Arena, whose outer shell glows red on match nights.","fact":"In Germany the club is called the Rekordmeister — record holder for national championship titles.","wiki_en":"FC Bayern Munich"},{"name":"Borussia Dortmund","emoji":"💛","meta":["football","Dortmund, Germany","1909"],"description":"The club from the Ruhr is famous for one of the most imposing stands in world football — the Yellow Wall, filled by tens of thousands of fans in black and yellow. Home is Signal Iduna Park.","fact":"The south stand of the club's stadium holds so many standing spectators that it is reckoned the largest in Europe.","wiki_en":"Borussia Dortmund"},{"name":"Juventus","emoji":"🦓","meta":["football","Turin, Italy","1897"],"description":"The Turin club in black and white stripes, nicknamed the Old Lady, is among the most decorated in the history of Italian football. For decades it has gathered world-class players into its squad.","fact":"The black-and-white striped kit is so recognisable that fans compare it to a zebra's coat.","wiki_en":"Juventus FC"},{"name":"AC Milan","emoji":"⭐","meta":["football","Milan, Italy","1899"],"description":"One of the two great Milan clubs, playing in red and black and known as the Rossoneri. Through the twentieth century and into the twenty-first it won the European Cup and the Champions League many times over.","fact":"The club kept the English spelling \"Milan\" rather than the Italian \"Milano\", in tribute to its British founders.","wiki_en":"AC Milan"},{"name":"Inter Milan","emoji":"🐍","meta":["football","Milan, Italy","1908"],"description":"Milan's second club, in blue and black, was founded by players who broke away from AC Milan because they wanted a team open to foreigners. It is known to everyone as the Nerazzurri.","fact":"The biscione, the serpent that is a historic emblem of Milan and of the ruling Visconti family, has appeared on the club's shirt more than once.","wiki_en":"Inter Milan"},{"name":"Napoli","emoji":"🌋","meta":["football","Naples, Italy","1926"],"description":"The club of the great city of southern Italy, where football is a central part of civic identity. Napoli made its name in the late 1980s, when Diego Maradona wore its shirt and turned the team into a title winner.","fact":"After Maradona's death in 2020 the club's home stadium was renamed in his honour.","wiki_en":"SSC Napoli"},{"name":"AS Roma","emoji":"🐺","meta":["football","Rome, Italy","1927"],"description":"The club of the Italian capital carries on its badge the she-wolf that, in legend, suckled Rome's founders Romulus and Remus. It plays in deep red and yellow, the colours of the city itself.","fact":"The club was created by merging several Roman football teams, to give the capital a stronger side in the Italian championship.","wiki_en":"AS Roma"},{"name":"Paris Saint-Germain","emoji":"🗼","meta":["football","Paris, France","1970"],"description":"The club of the French capital became one of the richest in world football after passing into Qatari ownership in the early 2010s, assembling a squad full of global stars. It plays in dark blue with a red stripe.","fact":"For all its status as a capital-city giant, the club was founded comparatively late — only in 1970.","wiki_en":"Paris Saint-Germain F.C."},{"name":"Ajax","emoji":"⚔️","meta":["football","Amsterdam, Netherlands","1900"],"description":"The Amsterdam club is named after a hero of Greek myth and is known worldwide for the youth academy out of which the style called Total Football grew. It plays in white with a broad red band down the shirt.","fact":"Johan Cruyff, Dennis Bergkamp and dozens of other world stars came through the club's academy.","wiki_en":"AFC Ajax"},{"name":"Benfica","emoji":"🦅","meta":["football","Lisbon, Portugal","1904"],"description":"Portugal's best-supported club carries an eagle on its crest and plays in red. In the middle of the twentieth century, with Eusébio in the side, it won the European Cup twice in successive seasons.","fact":"Before home games at the Estádio da Luz a live eagle traditionally circles above the stands.","wiki_en":"S.L. Benfica"},{"name":"Porto","emoji":"🐉","meta":["football","Porto, Portugal","1893"],"description":"The club from Portugal's second city is nicknamed the Dragons after the beast on its crest. In 2004, with a young coach by the name of José Mourinho in charge, it went and won the Champions League.","fact":"Porto's 2004 Champions League win launched José Mourinho's coaching career at Europe's biggest clubs.","wiki_en":"FC Porto"},{"name":"Celtic","emoji":"☘️","meta":["football","Glasgow, Scotland","1887"],"description":"Founded by the Irish Catholic community of Glasgow, Celtic are the sworn rivals of Rangers in the Old Firm derby. In 1967 they became the first British club to win the European Cup.","fact":"The squad that won the 1967 European Cup was made up almost entirely of players born in and around Glasgow.","wiki_en":"Celtic F.C."},{"name":"Galatasaray","emoji":"🦁","meta":["football","Istanbul, Turkey","1905"],"description":"One of the leading clubs of Istanbul and of Turkey, with a lion as its symbol and \"Cim Bom\" as its best-known nickname. Its home games are famous for deafening support and a sea of yellow and red scarves.","fact":"In 2000 the club became the first Turkish side to win a European trophy, the UEFA Cup.","wiki_en":"Galatasaray S.K."},{"name":"Fenerbahçe","emoji":"🐤","meta":["football","Istanbul, Turkey","1907"],"description":"The Istanbul club plays in yellow and navy blue and its supporters are known as the Canaries. Fenerbahçe is the historic rival of Galatasaray and Beşiktaş, and those city derbies are the loudest fixtures in Turkish football.","fact":"The club was founded while the Ottoman Empire still stood, when starting a sports society required special permission from the authorities.","wiki_en":"Fenerbahçe S.K."},{"name":"Boca Juniors","emoji":"🍫","meta":["football","Buenos Aires, Argentina","1905"],"description":"The club of La Boca, the port district of Buenos Aires, founded by Italian immigrants. Its stadium, La Bombonera, took its name from a shape that recalls a chocolate box, with stands rising almost vertically over the pitch.","fact":"Diego Maradona played here, and fans regard Boca and River Plate as the great antagonists of Argentine football.","wiki_en":"Boca Juniors"},{"name":"River Plate","emoji":"💰","meta":["football","Buenos Aires, Argentina","1901"],"description":"One of Argentina's most decorated clubs, nicknamed Los Millonarios for the lavish transfers it was already paying for star players back in the 1930s. It is the sworn rival of Boca Juniors in the Superclásico.","fact":"River Plate against Boca Juniors is reckoned one of the most highly charged derbies in world football.","wiki_en":"Club Atlético River Plate"},{"name":"Flamengo","emoji":"❤️","meta":["football","Rio de Janeiro, Brazil","1895"],"description":"The club with the biggest following in Brazil plays in red and black hoops. Its football section was set up early in the twentieth century and quickly won over Rio de Janeiro — the club itself began life on the water, as a rowing society.","fact":"By various estimates the club has one of the largest fan bases of any sports team in South America.","wiki_en":"Clube de Regatas do Flamengo"},{"name":"Santos","emoji":"🐟","meta":["football","Santos, Brazil","1912"],"description":"The club of the Brazilian port city of Santos made its name in the late 1950s and the 1960s, when a young Pelé wore its white shirt and the team toured the world as an attraction in its own right.","fact":"It was for Santos that Pelé scored most of his club goals and became one of football's great legends.","wiki_en":"Santos FC"},{"name":"Inter Miami","emoji":"🌴","meta":["football","Miami, USA","2018"],"description":"A young club in the American MLS, co-owned by David Beckham. It became known around the world in 2023, when Lionel Messi signed for the team and turned a recent expansion side into one of the most watched in North America.","fact":"Messi's move to the club in 2023 sent global interest in MLS sharply upward.","wiki_en":"Inter Miami CF"},{"name":"Al Nassr","emoji":"🌙","meta":["football","Riyadh, Saudi Arabia","1955"],"description":"One of Saudi Arabia's leading clubs, playing in yellow and blue in Riyadh. It drew the attention of the whole football world in 2023 by signing Cristiano Ronaldo, the first of a string of big names brought to the Saudi league.","fact":"Ronaldo's move to the club was one of the most talked-about transfers in the history of world football.","wiki_en":"Al-Nassr FC"},{"name":"Spartak Moscow","emoji":"♦️","meta":["football","Moscow, Russia","1922"],"description":"One of the most popular football clubs in Russia, known as \"the people's team\" for the following it has right across the country. Its emblem is the red and white diamond worn on the chest of the shirt.","fact":"The club plays its home games at its own stadium in the Tushino district of Moscow, opened in 2014.","wiki_en":"FC Spartak Moscow"},{"name":"CSKA Moscow (football)","emoji":"🐴","meta":["football","Moscow, Russia","1911"],"description":"The Moscow club grew out of the army sports society, which is where its name comes from. Fans nicknamed the team \"the Horses\", after the mounted rider that appeared on an older version of the crest.","fact":"In 2005 the club became the first Russian side to win a European trophy, the UEFA Cup.","wiki_en":"PFC CSKA Moscow"},{"name":"Zenit Saint Petersburg","emoji":"⚓","meta":["football","Saint Petersburg, Russia","1925"],"description":"The leading football club of Saint Petersburg grew out of the works team of a local metals plant. Its badge carries the little ship from the spire of the Admiralty, one of the city's symbols, and the team plays in sky blue and white.","fact":"In 2008 the club won the UEFA Cup and the UEFA Super Cup — a rare international run for a Russian team.","wiki_en":"FC Zenit Saint Petersburg"},{"name":"Lokomotiv Moscow","emoji":"🚂","meta":["football","Moscow, Russia","1922"],"description":"The Moscow club was set up with the backing of the railway ministry, which explains both its name and all its railway imagery. It plays in red and green and counts as one of the oldest clubs in the capital.","fact":"Fans often call the club's home ground Cherkizovo, after the district of Moscow where it stands.","wiki_en":"FC Lokomotiv Moscow"},{"name":"Dynamo Moscow (football)","emoji":"🔷","meta":["football","Moscow, Russia","1923"],"description":"One of the oldest football clubs in Moscow, tied from the start to the sports society of the Soviet police and security services. Supporters call the team the white-and-blues, after the colours of the shirt.","fact":"Dynamo was one of the few Soviet sports societies that brought clubs together right across the country under one name and one badge.","wiki_en":"FC Dynamo Moscow"},{"name":"Krasnodar","emoji":"🌳","meta":["football","Krasnodar, Russia","2008"],"description":"One of the youngest clubs in the Russian Premier League, created by a local businessman and known for leaning on its own football academy. Beside the ground he laid out a large city park that shares the club's name.","fact":"Krasnodar Park, built with the club owner's money, has become one of the city's main attractions.","wiki_en":"FC Krasnodar"},{"name":"Rubin Kazan","emoji":"💎","meta":["football","Kazan, Russia","1958"],"description":"The club from Kazan took the Russian championship twice in a row at the end of the 2000s under coach Kurban Berdyev. It plays in white and green and was for years Tatarstan's main representative in the top division.","fact":"Beating Barcelona at Camp Nou in the 2009 Champions League brought Rubin loud European fame.","wiki_en":"FC Rubin Kazan"},{"name":"Shakhtar Donetsk","emoji":"⛏️","meta":["football","Donetsk, Ukraine","1936"],"description":"The club of the Donbas coal region, whose name and badge both point to the mining that shaped it — shakhtar means \"miner\". A repeat Ukrainian champion, it won the UEFA Cup in 2009.","fact":"Since fighting broke out in the Donbas the club has been forced for years to play its home games in other Ukrainian cities.","wiki_en":"FC Shakhtar Donetsk"},{"name":"Dynamo Kyiv","emoji":"🔷","meta":["football","Kyiv, Ukraine","1927"],"description":"Ukraine's leading football club through the Soviet and post-Soviet decades, at its strongest in Europe under coach Valeriy Lobanovskyi. It won the European Cup Winners' Cup in 1975 and again in 1986.","fact":"Lobanovskyi's coaching methods at Dynamo Kyiv were ahead of their time and were studied by specialists all over the world.","wiki_en":"FC Dynamo Kyiv"},{"name":"Los Angeles Lakers","emoji":"💜","meta":["basketball","Los Angeles, USA","1947"],"description":"One of the most decorated clubs in the NBA, with Magic Johnson, Kobe Bryant and LeBron James all wearing its colours at different times. The Lakers name survives from the club's early days in lake-covered Minnesota.","fact":"The club was founded in Minneapolis and moved to Los Angeles only in 1960, but kept the old \"lake\" name.","wiki_en":"Los Angeles Lakers"},{"name":"Boston Celtics","emoji":"🍀","meta":["basketball","Boston, USA","1946"],"description":"One of the oldest and most decorated clubs in the NBA, named for the Irish settlers who made up a large share of Boston's population. The team's symbol is the shamrock, and its championship banners hang above a famous parquet floor.","fact":"The club is one of the great historical rivals of the Los Angeles Lakers in NBA finals across several decades.","wiki_en":"Boston Celtics"},{"name":"Chicago Bulls","emoji":"🐂","meta":["basketball","Chicago, USA","1966"],"description":"The club became famous worldwide in the 1990s, with six championship titles won under coach Phil Jackson and led by Michael Jordan. Home is the United Center in Chicago, where a statue of Jordan stands at the entrance.","fact":"That team of the 1990s is the subject of the documentary series \"The Last Dance\", a worldwide hit.","wiki_en":"Chicago Bulls"},{"name":"Golden State Warriors","emoji":"💦","meta":["basketball","San Francisco, USA","1946"],"description":"The team from the San Francisco Bay Area was reborn in the 2010s, when the shooting pair of Stephen Curry and Klay Thompson changed how basketball thinks about the three-point shot. The club took several NBA titles that decade.","fact":"Fans nicknamed Curry and Thompson the Splash Brothers for their accuracy from long range.","wiki_en":"Golden State Warriors"},{"name":"Miami Heat","emoji":"🔥","meta":["basketball","Miami, USA","1988"],"description":"The Florida club made its name in the early 2010s, when three superstars — LeBron James, Dwyane Wade and Chris Bosh — came together to form a famous attacking trio. The team plays in black and red.","fact":"The club's mascot is a flaming character called Burnie, one of the most recognisable mascots in the NBA.","wiki_en":"Miami Heat"},{"name":"New York Knicks","emoji":"🗽","meta":["basketball","New York, USA","1946"],"description":"One of the oldest clubs in the NBA, playing at the iconic Madison Square Garden in the middle of Manhattan. Long years without a championship have not stopped the team being one of the most popular and commercially successful in the league.","fact":"The club's home arena has long been billed as \"the world's most famous arena\".","wiki_en":"New York Knicks"},{"name":"San Antonio Spurs","emoji":"🤠","meta":["basketball","San Antonio, USA","1967"],"description":"Under coach Gregg Popovich the Texas club built one of the steadiest systems in NBA history, winning several championships with the centre Tim Duncan. The team is known for disciplined, collective basketball.","fact":"Tim Duncan spent his whole career — nineteen seasons — at one club, a rarity for a star of that standing.","wiki_en":"San Antonio Spurs"},{"name":"Dallas Mavericks","emoji":"🐴","meta":["basketball","Dallas, USA","1980"],"description":"The Texas club's great moment came in 2011, when it won the NBA championship led by the German forward Dirk Nowitzki, one of the finest shooters in the league's history. Nowitzki spent his entire career with the team.","fact":"Dirk Nowitzki was the first European to be named most valuable player of the NBA regular season.","wiki_en":"Dallas Mavericks"},{"name":"Montreal Canadiens","emoji":"🍁","meta":["ice hockey","Montreal, Canada","1909"],"description":"The oldest club in the NHL and one of the emblems of French-speaking Quebec, holder of more Stanley Cups than any other team in the league. Fans and reporters alike fondly call it the Habs.","fact":"The nickname Habs is short for les Habitants, the name given to the first French settlers of Canada.","wiki_en":"Montreal Canadiens"},{"name":"Toronto Maple Leafs","emoji":"🍁","meta":["ice hockey","Toronto, Canada","1917"],"description":"One of the six original NHL clubs and one of the most valuable franchises in world hockey, despite a long run of seasons without the Stanley Cup. Its symbol is the maple leaf, Canada's chief emblem.","fact":"No other club among the NHL's Original Six has gone longer without winning the Stanley Cup.","wiki_en":"Toronto Maple Leafs"},{"name":"Detroit Red Wings","emoji":"🛞","meta":["ice hockey","Detroit, USA","1926"],"description":"One of the six original NHL clubs and a repeat Stanley Cup winner, above all in the late 1990s and early 2000s. Detroit's devotion to the game earned the city the nickname Hockeytown.","fact":"At home games fans traditionally throw an octopus onto the ice — a good-luck custom that began back in the middle of the twentieth century.","wiki_en":"Detroit Red Wings"},{"name":"Chicago Blackhawks","emoji":"🪶","meta":["ice hockey","Chicago, USA","1926"],"description":"One of the six original NHL clubs, it lived through a second golden age in the 2010s, winning the Stanley Cup three times in six years. Jonathan Toews and Patrick Kane led that team.","fact":"The club's badge, the profile of a Native American chief, is one of the most recognisable in world sport.","wiki_en":"Chicago Blackhawks"},{"name":"New York Rangers","emoji":"🗽","meta":["ice hockey","New York, USA","1926"],"description":"One of the six original NHL clubs, it went decade after decade without the Stanley Cup until it finally won in 1994, to the delight of all Manhattan. Home is Madison Square Garden.","fact":"Fans had waited fifty-four years for that 1994 win and greeted it with banners reading \"Now I can die in peace\".","wiki_en":"New York Rangers"},{"name":"Pittsburgh Penguins","emoji":"🐧","meta":["ice hockey","Pittsburgh, USA","1967"],"description":"The club has won the NHL several times over thanks to outstanding centres from two different eras — first Mario Lemieux, then Sidney Crosby. Both are counted among the finest players in the league's history.","fact":"Mario Lemieux not only played for the club but later became a co-owner, helping it out of a financial crisis.","wiki_en":"Pittsburgh Penguins"},{"name":"Washington Capitals","emoji":"🦅","meta":["ice hockey","Washington, USA","1974"],"description":"The club of the American capital spent years unable to reach the top of the NHL, until in 2018 it won its first ever Stanley Cup with the Russian forward Alexander Ovechkin leading the way.","fact":"Playing for the club, Ovechkin became the NHL's all-time record holder for career goals.","wiki_en":"Washington Capitals"},{"name":"Edmonton Oilers","emoji":"🛢️","meta":["ice hockey","Edmonton, Canada","1972"],"description":"The club had its golden age in the 1980s, when Wayne Gretzky was the brightest name on the roster and the team won the Stanley Cup several times. Its name nods to the oil industry of Alberta.","fact":"The trade that sent Gretzky away in 1988 was a national shock in Canada, debated in all seriousness even in parliament.","wiki_en":"Edmonton Oilers"},{"name":"Boston Bruins","emoji":"🐻","meta":["ice hockey","Boston, USA","1924"],"description":"The oldest of the NHL's American clubs and one of the league's original six, it is remembered above all for the great defenceman Bobby Orr. Boston play in black and gold and are among the most storied franchises in the league.","fact":"The photograph of Bobby Orr flying through the air after his winning goal in the 1970 Stanley Cup is one of the most recognisable images in hockey.","wiki_en":"Boston Bruins"},{"name":"CSKA Moscow (ice hockey)","emoji":"🔴","meta":["ice hockey","Moscow, Russia","1946"],"description":"The dominant hockey club of the Soviet era, for years the backbone of the USSR national team and all but unbeatable in the domestic championship. Legendary players passed through it, Valeri Kharlamov among them.","fact":"For decades the USSR national hockey team was drawn almost entirely from CSKA's roster.","wiki_en":"HC CSKA Moscow"},{"name":"SKA Saint Petersburg","emoji":"🔵","meta":["ice hockey","Saint Petersburg, Russia","1946"],"description":"One of the most decorated clubs of the modern KHL, twice winner of the Gagarin Cup in the second half of the 2010s. Its home games are played at one of the largest ice arenas in Europe.","fact":"The club draws some of the biggest home crowds of any team in the KHL.","wiki_en":"SKA Saint Petersburg"},{"name":"Ak Bars Kazan","emoji":"🐆","meta":["ice hockey","Kazan, Russia","1956"],"description":"The leading hockey club of Tatarstan, a several-time winner of the Gagarin Cup in the KHL. Its name is Tatar for \"white leopard\", the old Turkic term for the snow leopard, an animal the region has long claimed as its own.","fact":"The snow leopard that gave the club its name also appears on the state coat of arms of Tatarstan.","wiki_en":"Ak Bars Kazan"},{"name":"Metallurg Magnitogorsk","emoji":"⚙️","meta":["ice hockey","Magnitogorsk, Russia","1955"],"description":"The club of an industrial city in the Urals, closely tied to the local steel works that gave it its name — metallurg means \"metalworker\". It has won the Russian championship more than once and taken the KHL's Gagarin Cup.","fact":"Evgeni Malkin, later a star in the NHL, began his career in the club's Magnitogorsk school.","wiki_en":"Metallurg Magnitogorsk"},{"name":"Avangard Omsk","emoji":"🚀","meta":["ice hockey","Omsk, Russia","1950"],"description":"One of the oldest hockey clubs in Siberia and a winner of the Gagarin Cup in the KHL. For some seasons the team played part of its home schedule near Moscow while its own arena was being rebuilt.","fact":"The club won the Gagarin Cup in 2021, while based in Balashikha outside Moscow because a new arena was under construction.","wiki_en":"Avangard Omsk"},{"name":"Salavat Yulaev","emoji":"🗡️","meta":["ice hockey","Ufa, Russia","1961"],"description":"The hockey club of Bashkortostan, named after the national hero Salavat Yulaev, a warrior and a poet. The team took the Russian championship in 2008 and the KHL's Gagarin Cup in 2011.","fact":"The club is named for Salavat Yulaev, a comrade of Yemelyan Pugachev revered in Bashkortostan as a folk hero.","wiki_en":"Salavat Yulaev Ufa"},{"name":"Lokomotiv Yaroslavl","emoji":"🚂","meta":["ice hockey","Yaroslavl, Russia","1959"],"description":"A club with a long history, marked by one of the worst tragedies in sport: in 2011 almost the whole team died in a plane crash on the way to the first match of the season. The club was rebuilt from scratch and plays on in the KHL.","fact":"After the crash the club sat out a KHL season and returned to the league with a completely new roster.","wiki_en":"Lokomotiv Yaroslavl"},{"name":"Dallas Cowboys","emoji":"⭐","meta":["American football","Dallas, USA","1960"],"description":"One of the most valuable and most recognisable teams in the NFL, which decades of popularity earned the unofficial nickname \"America's Team\". It plays in white and blue with a star on the helmet.","fact":"The club picked up the nickname America's Team back in the 1970s, thanks to the huge television audience it drew across the country.","wiki_en":"Dallas Cowboys"},{"name":"New England Patriots","emoji":"🏈","meta":["American football","Foxborough, USA","1960"],"description":"The club built a genuine dynasty through the 2000s and the 2010s on the partnership of coach Bill Belichick and quarterback Tom Brady, who between them won six Super Bowls for New England.","fact":"The Belichick–Brady pairing is reckoned the most successful coach and quarterback duo in the whole history of the NFL.","wiki_en":"New England Patriots"},{"name":"Kansas City Chiefs","emoji":"🏹","meta":["American football","Kansas City, USA","1960"],"description":"The club found a new golden age in the 2020s behind quarterback Patrick Mahomes, winning the Super Bowl several times. Its home ground, Arrowhead Stadium, is famous for one of the loudest crowds in sport.","fact":"Arrowhead Stadium has repeatedly recorded some of the highest crowd noise levels of any sports arena in the world.","wiki_en":"Kansas City Chiefs"},{"name":"Green Bay Packers","emoji":"🧀","meta":["American football","Green Bay, USA","1919"],"description":"A club unique in big American sport: a small-town team owned not by a businessman but by thousands of supporter shareholders. Its history was made famous by the legendary coach Vince Lombardi.","fact":"The Packers are the only non-profit, entirely fan-owned franchise among the major professional leagues of the United States.","wiki_en":"Green Bay Packers"},{"name":"San Francisco 49ers","emoji":"⛏️","meta":["American football","San Francisco, USA","1946"],"description":"The dominant club of the late 1980s and early 1990s under coach Bill Walsh and quarterback Joe Montana, a five-time Super Bowl winner. The name refers to the gold prospectors of 1849.","fact":"Joe Montana never lost a Super Bowl he took the field in — he won all four of them.","wiki_en":"San Francisco 49ers"},{"name":"New York Yankees","emoji":"⚾","meta":["baseball","New York, USA","1901"],"description":"The most decorated club in the history of Major League Baseball, with legends such as Babe Ruth and Mickey Mantle on its roll. The pinstriped uniform and the interlocked NY monogram are among the most recognisable symbols in American sport.","fact":"The club has won more World Series titles than any other MLB team, by a wide margin over its nearest rivals.","wiki_en":"New York Yankees"},{"name":"Boston Red Sox","emoji":"🧦","meta":["baseball","Boston, USA","1901"],"description":"The historic rival of the New York Yankees went decades without a World Series title — a drought fans called the Curse of the Bambino. In 2004 the club finally broke that long run without a championship.","fact":"Fans tied the 2004 win to the lifting of the Curse of the Bambino, which began when Babe Ruth was sold to the Yankees in 1920.","wiki_en":"Boston Red Sox"},{"name":"Los Angeles Dodgers","emoji":"💙","meta":["baseball","Los Angeles, USA","1883"],"description":"A club whose story began in Brooklyn, where in 1947 Jackie Robinson made his debut for it — the first Black player in the modern history of Major League Baseball. The team moved to Los Angeles in 1958.","fact":"Jackie Robinson's debut for the club in 1947 broke a racial barrier that had stood for decades in top-level American baseball.","wiki_en":"Los Angeles Dodgers"},{"name":"Scuderia Ferrari","emoji":"🐎","meta":["Formula 1","Maranello, Italy","1929"],"description":"The oldest and most successful team in Formula 1, founded by Enzo Ferrari and inseparable from the Italian carmaker of the same name. Its symbol is the prancing horse on a yellow shield.","fact":"Ferrari is the only team to have entered every season of the Formula 1 world championship since it began in 1950.","wiki_en":"Scuderia Ferrari"},{"name":"McLaren F1","emoji":"🧡","meta":["Formula 1","Woking, United Kingdom","1963"],"description":"One of the most successful British teams in Formula 1, which across different decades carried drivers such as Ayrton Senna and Lewis Hamilton to world titles. It was founded by the New Zealand racer Bruce McLaren.","fact":"The team's founder, Bruce McLaren, was killed in 1970 while testing one of his own racing cars.","wiki_en":"McLaren"},{"name":"Red Bull Racing","emoji":"🐂","meta":["Formula 1","Milton Keynes, United Kingdom","2005"],"description":"Built around the energy drink brand, the team became one of the strongest in Formula 1 — first with Sebastian Vettel in the early 2010s, then with Max Verstappen in the 2020s, taking drivers' and constructors' titles in both spells.","fact":"The team came into being after Red Bull bought the rights and assets of the former Jaguar Racing outfit.","wiki_en":"Red Bull Racing"},{"name":"Mercedes F1","emoji":"⭐","meta":["Formula 1","Brackley, United Kingdom","2010"],"description":"The works team of the German carmaker dominated Formula 1's hybrid era from 2014 to 2020 alongside Lewis Hamilton, taking one constructors' championship after another in an unbroken run.","fact":"The silver paintwork earned the team the nickname Silver Arrows, a nod to the pre-war Mercedes racing cars.","wiki_en":"Mercedes-AMG Petronas F1 Team"},{"name":"Williams F1","emoji":"🔷","meta":["Formula 1","Grove, United Kingdom","1977"],"description":"An independent British team founded by Frank Williams, it won world championships several times over in the 1980s and the 1990s, with drivers such as Nigel Mansell and Damon Hill at the wheel of its cars.","fact":"Williams is one of the few teams in Formula 1 still carrying the surname of its founder.","wiki_en":"Williams Racing"},{"name":"Brazil national football team","emoji":"💛","meta":["football","Brazil","1914"],"description":"The only national team to have won the football World Cup five times, and the only one to have played in every single tournament. It gave the world players such as Pelé, Ronaldo and Ronaldinho.","fact":"The yellow shirt has become so recognisable that in many countries it stands for Brazilian football itself.","wiki_en":"Brazil national football team"},{"name":"Argentina national football team","emoji":"🩵","meta":["football","Argentina","1901"],"description":"A three-time world champion that gave football both Diego Maradona and Lionel Messi. The team plays in white and sky-blue stripes, which is where the nickname La Albiceleste — \"the white and sky blue\" — comes from.","fact":"The third and long-awaited world title came in 2022, with Lionel Messi wearing the captain's armband.","wiki_en":"Argentina national football team"},{"name":"Germany national football team","emoji":"⚫","meta":["football","Germany","1908"],"description":"One of the most decorated sides in World Cup history, traditionally admired for disciplined and efficient football. It has won both the World Cup and the European Championship several times over.","fact":"In the world's press the German side is often called simply \"the team\" — Die Mannschaft.","wiki_en":"Germany national football team"},{"name":"France national football team","emoji":"🐓","meta":["football","France","1904"],"description":"A two-time world champion, best remembered for winning the tournament at home in 1998 with Zinedine Zidane leading the side. The team is known as Les Bleus — \"the blues\" — after the colour of its shirt.","fact":"The cockerel on the badge is a traditional symbol of France: the Latin gallus meant both \"Gaul\" and \"rooster\".","wiki_en":"France national football team"},{"name":"Italy national football team","emoji":"🔵","meta":["football","Italy","1910"],"description":"A four-time world champion, famed for a solid defensive school known as catenaccio. The blue of the shirt, which gave the team its name the Azzurri, comes not from the Italian flag but from the arms of the House of Savoy.","fact":"Italy won the World Cup twice in a row, in 1934 and 1938, before the Second World War.","wiki_en":"Italy national football team"},{"name":"Spain national football team","emoji":"❤️","meta":["football","Spain","1920"],"description":"The side had a golden age between 2008 and 2012, winning two European Championships and a World Cup in a row with its distinctive tiki-taka style of play. Fans call the team La Roja — \"the red one\".","fact":"Spain inherited much of that tiki-taka style from Barcelona, whose players formed the core of the squad.","wiki_en":"Spain national football team"},{"name":"England national football team","emoji":"🦁","meta":["football","England","1872"],"description":"The national team of the country where the rules of modern football were written, and yet a World Cup winner only once — at home in 1966. The three lions on the badge give the side its nickname.","fact":"That 1966 victory remains the only major men's international trophy in the history of English football.","wiki_en":"England national football team"},{"name":"Russia national football team","emoji":"🐻","meta":["football","Russia","1992"],"description":"The side has played under this name since 1992, inheriting part of the Soviet football tradition after the break-up of the USSR. Its best showing at a European Championship was the semi-final in 2008.","fact":"At the home World Cup in 2018 the team reached the quarter-finals, knocking Spain out on penalties along the way.","wiki_en":"Russia national football team"},{"name":"Soviet Union national ice hockey team","emoji":"🔴","meta":["ice hockey","USSR","1954"],"description":"One of the most dominant teams in the history of world hockey, winning Olympic tournaments and world championships for decades on end. It took gold at its very first world championship, on debut in 1954.","fact":"The USSR's shock defeat by a student team from the United States at the 1980 Olympics went down in history as the Miracle on Ice.","wiki_en":"Soviet Union national ice hockey team"},{"name":"Canada national ice hockey team","emoji":"🍁","meta":["ice hockey","Canada","1920"],"description":"The national team of the country regarded as the home of modern hockey long set the standard for the sport worldwide. The series of matches against the USSR in 1972 holds a special place in its history.","fact":"In Canada the 1972 Summit Series against the Soviet team is still remembered as one of the most dramatic sporting contests of the twentieth century.","wiki_en":"Canada men's national ice hockey team"},{"name":"United States national basketball team","emoji":"🏀","meta":["basketball","USA","1936"],"description":"The most decorated national side in world basketball, which made its Olympic debut in 1936. It became especially famous in 1992, when serving NBA stars were sent to the Games for the first time.","fact":"The 1992 squad, known as the Dream Team, with Michael Jordan and Magic Johnson, is regarded as the strongest team in basketball history.","wiki_en":"United States men's national basketball team"},{"name":"All Blacks","emoji":"⚫","meta":["rugby","New Zealand","1892"],"description":"New Zealand's rugby side is reckoned the most successful national team in the history of the sport. Before every match the players perform the haka, the traditional Māori dance that challenges the opponent.","fact":"The All Blacks have won the Rugby World Cup more than once, including on home soil in 2011.","wiki_en":"New Zealand national rugby union team"},{"name":"Harlem Globetrotters","emoji":"🌎","meta":["basketball","USA","1926"],"description":"Not a sports team in the ordinary sense but a touring show that mixes real basketball skill with circus tricks and comedy routines. The Globetrotters have taken their exhibition games to more than a hundred countries.","fact":"The team's signature tune, a whistled \"Sweet Georgia Brown\", is recognised the world over.","wiki_en":"Harlem Globetrotters"},{"name":"Pittsburgh Steelers","emoji":"🏈","meta":["American football","Pittsburgh, USA","1933"],"description":"One of the most decorated clubs in the NFL, known for aggressive defence and for devoted fans who wave yellow Terrible Towels. Its black and gold colours point to Pittsburgh's steel industry.","fact":"The club's logo is the only badge in the NFL applied to just one side of the playing helmet.","wiki_en":"Pittsburgh Steelers"},{"name":"Philadelphia Eagles","emoji":"🦅","meta":["American football","Philadelphia, USA","1933"],"description":"The team took its name from the eagle on the badge of the National Recovery Administration, a symbol of the New Deal in 1930s America. The Eagles are known for one of the most passionate and demanding fan bases in the league, and play at Lincoln Financial Field.","fact":"The club's mascot, Swoop the eagle, appears at every home game and is one of the best known in the NFL.","wiki_en":"Philadelphia Eagles"},{"name":"Buffalo Bills","emoji":"🦬","meta":["American football","Buffalo, USA","1960"],"description":"The club from upstate New York is named after the frontiersman Buffalo Bill Cody. It plays at an open stadium famous for brutal snow games, and its fans are known for the tradition of jumping through folding tables before kick-off.","fact":"Bills supporters are called the Bills Mafia for their loud and sometimes extreme pre-game rituals.","wiki_en":"Buffalo Bills"},{"name":"Seattle Seahawks","emoji":"🦅","meta":["American football","Seattle, USA","1974"],"description":"The team's name comes from the osprey, the sea hawk common to the Pacific Northwest of the United States. Its fans are so loud they have been dubbed the 12th Man, and the home stadium counts as one of the noisiest in the league.","fact":"In honour of its supporters the club officially retired the number 12 — no player is given it.","wiki_en":"Seattle Seahawks"},{"name":"Los Angeles Rams","emoji":"🐏","meta":["American football","Los Angeles, USA","1936"],"description":"A club whose story began in Cleveland, before moves to Los Angeles, then St. Louis, then back to California. The team is known for the curled horns on its helmets — one of the first club logos in NFL history.","fact":"The Rams were among the first clubs in the league to put an emblem on their playing helmets, back in the 1940s.","wiki_en":"Los Angeles Rams"},{"name":"Miami Dolphins","emoji":"🐬","meta":["American football","Miami, USA","1966"],"description":"The only team in NFL history to finish a season without a single defeat and go on to win the Super Bowl. The club plays in aqua and orange, colours that catch the mood of sunny Florida.","fact":"The Dolphins' perfect season of 1972 is still a one-off achievement in the history of the league.","wiki_en":"Miami Dolphins"},{"name":"Las Vegas Raiders","emoji":"☠️","meta":["American football","Las Vegas, USA","1960"],"description":"A club with pirate imagery and a reputation as one of the league's most rebellious teams, it has changed home city several times — from Oakland to Los Angeles and back, then on to Las Vegas. The silver and black kit is its calling card.","fact":"The club's badge — a skull in a football helmet with crossed swords behind it — has barely changed since the team was founded.","wiki_en":"Las Vegas Raiders"},{"name":"Denver Broncos","emoji":"🐎","meta":["American football","Denver, USA","1960"],"description":"The team is named after an unbroken mustang, a symbol of the American West. The Broncos remain the only club from Colorado playing in the top professional leagues of American football.","fact":"The rearing horse logo on the team's helmets has barely changed since the end of the 1990s.","wiki_en":"Denver Broncos"},{"name":"Philadelphia 76ers","emoji":"🏀","meta":["basketball","Philadelphia, USA","1946"],"description":"One of the oldest clubs in the NBA, it began life as the Syracuse Nationals, before the modern league even existed. The team plays in blue and red, echoing the American flag, and is famous for great players of several eras.","fact":"The name Sixers points to 1776, the year the Declaration of Independence was signed in Philadelphia.","wiki_en":"Philadelphia 76ers"},{"name":"Milwaukee Bucks","emoji":"🦌","meta":["basketball","Milwaukee, USA","1968"],"description":"The club from Wisconsin is named after the white-tailed deer often seen in the state's forests. It combines basketball tradition with a modern style of play, and its home arena counts as one of the most high-tech in the league.","fact":"The club's mascot, Bango, is known for acrobatic stunts before games, trampoline jumps included.","wiki_en":"Milwaukee Bucks"},{"name":"Phoenix Suns","emoji":"☀️","meta":["basketball","Phoenix, USA","1968"],"description":"The team from sunny Arizona takes its name and its orange and purple palette from the hot desert climate of the region. The Suns are known for flamboyant attacking line-ups and one of the most recognisable identities in the league.","fact":"The club's mascot is the Gorilla, which by the well-known story began with a courier in a gorilla suit who once delivered a greetings telegram to a game.","wiki_en":"Phoenix Suns"},{"name":"Brooklyn Nets","emoji":"🏀","meta":["basketball","New York, USA","1967"],"description":"The club began in the ABA as the New Jersey Americans and only moved to Brooklyn in the twenty-first century. Its minimal black-and-white kit has become one of the recognisable brands of the modern NBA.","fact":"Over its history the club has changed names several times: New Jersey Americans, New York Nets, New Jersey Nets and finally Brooklyn Nets.","wiki_en":"Brooklyn Nets"},{"name":"Denver Nuggets","emoji":"⛏️","meta":["basketball","Denver, USA","1967"],"description":"The club's name recalls the Colorado gold rush of the nineteenth century, when prospectors panned mountain rivers for nuggets. It is the state's only team in the NBA and one of the clubs that came over from the ABA.","fact":"The Nuggets' home arena stands more than a mile above sea level.","wiki_en":"Denver Nuggets"},{"name":"Oklahoma City Thunder","emoji":"⚡","meta":["basketball","Oklahoma City, USA","1967"],"description":"The club began as the Seattle SuperSonics and moved to Oklahoma only in 2008. The name Thunder refers to the frequent storms of this part of the United States, the region known as Tornado Alley.","fact":"The move from Seattle upset fans so much that the city spent years campaigning to get an NBA club back.","wiki_en":"Oklahoma City Thunder"},{"name":"Cleveland Cavaliers","emoji":"⚔️","meta":["basketball","Cleveland, USA","1970"],"description":"The club from Ohio takes its name from the cavaliers, the mounted gentlemen of old, a nod to the spirit of chivalry. More than once it has risen to stardom on the back of players who grew up in the same region.","fact":"The club's home arena sits right in the centre of Cleveland, not far from Lake Erie.","wiki_en":"Cleveland Cavaliers"},{"name":"Toronto Raptors","emoji":"🦖","meta":["basketball","Toronto, Canada","1995"],"description":"The only non-American club in NBA history to have won the championship. The name arrived on the wave of popularity of \"Jurassic Park\" and catches the mood of the mid-1990s, when the franchise was founded.","fact":"The square outside the arena, where fans watch games on a big screen in the open air, has been nicknamed Jurassic Park.","wiki_en":"Toronto Raptors"},{"name":"Chicago Cubs","emoji":"🐻","meta":["baseball","Chicago, USA","1876"],"description":"One of the oldest professional clubs in the United States, with a history going back to the century before last. For decade after decade the team was associated with the longest championship drought in American sport, broken only in the 2010s.","fact":"The club's home ground, Wrigley Field, is the second-oldest ballpark still in use in the American major leagues.","wiki_en":"Chicago Cubs"},{"name":"San Francisco Giants","emoji":"⚾","meta":["baseball","San Francisco, USA","1883"],"description":"The club was born in New York and moved to the west coast at the end of the 1950s, becoming one of the emblems of San Francisco. Its stadium is known for the view over the bay, where struck balls sometimes land.","fact":"Some of the home runs hit out of the Giants' ballpark fly straight into the waters of San Francisco Bay.","wiki_en":"San Francisco Giants"},{"name":"St. Louis Cardinals","emoji":"🐦","meta":["baseball","St. Louis, USA","1882"],"description":"The club's name came from the cardinal shade of red in its uniform; the cardinal bird became the emblem only later. The team is reckoned one of the most successful in baseball history and has one of the most devoted fan bases in the American Midwest.","fact":"Cardinals supporters are often called the best in the league for their quiet but deep knowledge of the game.","wiki_en":"St. Louis Cardinals"},{"name":"Atlanta Braves","emoji":"⚾","meta":["baseball","Atlanta, USA","1871"],"description":"One of the oldest continuously operating professional sports clubs in the United States, it began in Boston and passed through several cities before settling in Atlanta. The team is a notable part of the sporting culture of the American South.","fact":"Before settling in Atlanta the club played in Boston and in Milwaukee.","wiki_en":"Atlanta Braves"},{"name":"Houston Astros","emoji":"🚀","meta":["baseball","Houston, USA","1962"],"description":"The club's name is a nod to the NASA space centre just outside Houston. It was among the first teams in the league to play in a covered stadium with artificial turf — the famous Astrodome.","fact":"In the 1960s the club's home stadium was billed as the eighth wonder of the world for its innovative roof.","wiki_en":"Houston Astros"},{"name":"New York Islanders","emoji":"🏝️","meta":["ice hockey","New York, USA","1972"],"description":"The club plays not in Manhattan itself but out on Long Island, which is where its name comes from. In the late 1970s and early 1980s it lived through one of the most successful runs in the league's history.","fact":"In the early 1980s the Islanders won the NHL's main trophy several seasons in a row.","wiki_en":"New York Islanders"},{"name":"Philadelphia Flyers","emoji":"🏒","meta":["ice hockey","Philadelphia, USA","1967"],"description":"The club appeared during the NHL's big expansion at the end of the 1960s and quickly earned a reputation as one of the toughest and least yielding teams in the league, picking up the nickname the Broad Street Bullies for its physical style.","fact":"The nickname Broad Street Bullies came from the team's aggressive manner of play in the 1970s.","wiki_en":"Philadelphia Flyers"},{"name":"St. Louis Blues","emoji":"🎷","meta":["ice hockey","St. Louis, USA","1967"],"description":"The club's name comes from the famous song \"Saint Louis Blues\" and from the city's musical heritage as one of the cradles of blues and jazz. It was one of six teams added in the NHL's first big expansion.","fact":"The club won its first Stanley Cup only more than half a century after it was founded.","wiki_en":"St. Louis Blues"},{"name":"Colorado Avalanche","emoji":"❄️","meta":["ice hockey","Denver, USA","1972"],"description":"The club started out as the Quebec Nordiques in Canada and moved to Colorado in the mid-1990s, winning the Stanley Cup in its very first season in the new home. The name refers to the snow avalanches of the Rocky Mountains.","fact":"In its first season after the move to Denver the team took the league's main trophy, to the surprise of many.","wiki_en":"Colorado Avalanche"},{"name":"Vancouver Canucks","emoji":"🏒","meta":["ice hockey","Vancouver, Canada","1970"],"description":"The only Canadian club in the NHL on the country's west coast, its name comes from Canuck, the colloquial nickname for Canadians. The team is known for bold blue and green colours and for the killer whale on its badge.","fact":"The club's logo has changed several times, but the orca has remained the team's constant symbol since the 1990s.","wiki_en":"Vancouver Canucks"},{"name":"Tampa Bay Lightning","emoji":"⚡","meta":["ice hockey","Tampa, USA","1992"],"description":"One of the few hockey clubs to settle in the heat of Florida, which for a long time looked like an odd fit for the NHL. The name of the team refers to the frequent storms and lightning of the region.","fact":"For all Florida's subtropical climate, the team has won the Stanley Cup several times.","wiki_en":"Tampa Bay Lightning"},{"name":"Sevilla","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Seville, Spain","1890"],"description":"One of the oldest football clubs in Spain, known above all for its record in the Europa League, which it has won more often than any other side in the competition. Home games are played at the Ramón Sánchez-Pizjuán.","fact":"Sevilla holds the record for UEFA Europa League wins among all the clubs that have played in it.","wiki_en":"Sevilla FC"},{"name":"Valencia","emoji":"🍊","meta":["football","Valencia, Spain","1919"],"description":"The club comes from a region famous for its orange groves and plays at the Mestalla, one of the oldest stadiums in Spain. Valencia is one of the few Spanish clubs to have competed regularly with the country's giants and taken the league title.","fact":"The club's nickname Che comes from the Valencian exclamation \"che\", a marker of the local way of speaking.","wiki_en":"Valencia CF"},{"name":"Marseille","emoji":"⚓","meta":["football","Marseille, France","1899"],"description":"The club from France's largest port city is the only French side ever to win the Champions League. Marseille's supporters are reckoned among the most passionate and the loudest anywhere in Europe.","fact":"Olympique de Marseille remains, so far, the only French club to have lifted the European Cup.","wiki_en":"Olympique de Marseille"},{"name":"Lyon","emoji":"🦁","meta":["football","Lyon, France","1950"],"description":"The club's name sounds like the French word for lion, which is why a lion appears on its crest. In the early 2000s the team won seven French championships in a row, a record run for the country.","fact":"Lyon's women's team is one of the most decorated in the history of European women's football.","wiki_en":"Olympique Lyonnais"},{"name":"RB Leipzig","emoji":"🐂","meta":["football","Leipzig, Germany","2009"],"description":"One of the youngest clubs in the German Bundesliga, set up with the backing of an energy drink company. In a decade and a half the team climbed from Germany's lower divisions to regular European competition.","fact":"Formally the club's initials stand not for the sponsor's brand but for RasenBallsport — \"lawn ball sport\".","wiki_en":"RB Leipzig"},{"name":"Schalke 04","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Gelsenkirchen, Germany","1904"],"description":"The club comes from the coal-mining Ruhr, and its supporter culture is closely bound up with the working traditions of Gelsenkirchen. The figures in its name are the date of its founding — 1904.","fact":"Schalke fans sing their own unofficial club anthem before every home match.","wiki_en":"FC Schalke 04"},{"name":"Lazio","emoji":"🦅","meta":["football","Rome, Italy","1900"],"description":"One of Rome's two big clubs, its symbol is the eagle, a nod to the imperial insignia of ancient Rome. The team plays in sky blue and has a long-running rivalry with Roma in the derby of the capital.","fact":"The club's mascot is a live eagle named Olimpia, which circles the stands before matches.","wiki_en":"SS Lazio"},{"name":"Fiorentina","emoji":"🌸","meta":["football","Florence, Italy","1926"],"description":"The club's name and the lily on its badge both point to Florence — the \"city of flowers\" and the cradle of the Italian Renaissance. The team traditionally plays in purple, which sets it apart from every other club in Italy.","fact":"Purple is a rarity in world football, and it makes Fiorentina instantly recognisable.","wiki_en":"ACF Fiorentina"},{"name":"Sporting Lisbon","emoji":"🦁","meta":["football","Lisbon, Portugal","1906"],"description":"One of the three biggest clubs in Portugal, best known for a football academy that has raised a good number of world stars. Its symbol is a lion and its home colours are green and white.","fact":"The club's academy is reckoned one of the most productive in Europe for the number of graduates who reached the top level.","wiki_en":"Sporting CP"},{"name":"West Ham United","emoji":"⚒️","meta":["football","London, England","1895"],"description":"The London club grew out of the works team at a shipyard, which is where the nickname the Hammers comes from and why crossed hammers sit on the badge. It is known for bringing through talented English players.","fact":"The two crossed hammers on the crest point straight back to the club's origins among dockyard workers.","wiki_en":"West Ham United F.C."},{"name":"Everton","emoji":"🍬","meta":["football","Liverpool, England","1878"],"description":"One of the oldest football clubs in England and the original neighbour of Liverpool in the same city. The team picked up the nickname the Toffees because of a shop nearby that made toffee sweets.","fact":"The nickname Toffees comes straight from a historic toffee shop that stood near the ground.","wiki_en":"Everton F.C."},{"name":"Newcastle United","emoji":"🐦","meta":["football","Newcastle, England","1892"],"description":"A club with one of the most loyal fan bases in English football, whose supporters fill St James' Park whatever the team's position in the table. The nickname the Magpies comes from the black and white stripes of the shirt.","fact":"Fans and reporters liken the team's black and white striped kit to the plumage of a magpie.","wiki_en":"Newcastle United F.C."},{"name":"Aston Villa","emoji":"🦁","meta":["football","Birmingham, England","1874"],"description":"One of the founders of the English Football League, the oldest professional championship in the world. The club from Birmingham has been champion of England several times and once won the European Cup.","fact":"Aston Villa was among the clubs that founded the English Football League in 1888.","wiki_en":"Aston Villa F.C."},{"name":"Feyenoord","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Rotterdam, Netherlands","1908"],"description":"The club from the port city of Rotterdam is known for the working-class character of its supporters and for its famous stadium, De Kuip. In the early 1970s Feyenoord became the first Dutch club to win the European Cup.","fact":"Feyenoord got there ahead of the celebrated Ajax and became the first Dutch holder of Europe's main trophy.","wiki_en":"Feyenoord"},{"name":"Krylia Sovetov","emoji":"✈️","meta":["football","Samara, Russia","1942"],"description":"The club was founded during the Second World War at an aircraft factory, which is where its name — \"Wings of the Soviets\" — comes from. It plays its home games at the Samara Arena, built for the 2018 World Cup.","fact":"The club's name points straight back to the aircraft plant it was set up alongside.","wiki_en":"FC Krylia Sovetov Samara"},{"name":"Rostov","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Rostov-on-Don, Russia","1930"],"description":"The club from a city in southern Russia has had several notable runs in European competition, including a place in the Champions League group stage. It plays at a modern stadium on the bank of the Don.","fact":"In the 2016/17 Champions League group stage Rostov managed to beat Bayern Munich at home.","wiki_en":"FC Rostov"},{"name":"CSKA Moscow (basketball)","emoji":"🏀","meta":["basketball","Moscow, Russia","1923"],"description":"One of the most decorated basketball clubs in Europe and a repeat winner of the EuroLeague. The team was founded as part of a large army sports society in Moscow and remains the flagship of Russian basketball.","fact":"CSKA is one of the few European basketball clubs to have contended for the EuroLeague title decade after decade.","wiki_en":"PBC CSKA Moscow"},{"name":"Dynamo Moscow (ice hockey)","emoji":"🏒","meta":["ice hockey","Moscow, Russia","1946"],"description":"One of the oldest and most decorated hockey clubs in Russia, representing the Dynamo sports society. The team took the national championship many times in the Soviet era and again in the post-Soviet years.","fact":"The club's white and blue colours have stayed virtually unchanged through almost its entire history.","wiki_en":"HC Dynamo Moscow"},{"name":"Traktor Chelyabinsk","emoji":"🚜","meta":["ice hockey","Chelyabinsk, Russia","1947"],"description":"The club is named after the Chelyabinsk tractor works, alongside which the team was created. Traktor is known for one of the hottest and most devoted fan bases in Russian hockey, despite winning few titles.","fact":"The club's name is tied directly to the largest engineering plant in Chelyabinsk.","wiki_en":"HC Traktor Chelyabinsk"},{"name":"Ural Yekaterinburg","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Yekaterinburg, Russia","1930"],"description":"The club represents the largest city of the Urals region and spent many years in the top division of Russian football with mixed fortunes. It is known for the loyalty of its supporters despite modest results in recent seasons.","fact":"Until 2003 the club was called Uralmash, after a large engineering works in Yekaterinburg.","wiki_en":"FC Ural Yekaterinburg"},{"name":"Torpedo Nizhny Novgorod","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Nizhny Novgorod, Russia","1930"],"description":"The club is closely tied to the GAZ car plant, alongside which it was set up in the earliest days of Soviet football. Over its history the team has moved back and forth between the top and the lower divisions of Russian football.","fact":"In Soviet times several factory football clubs around the country carried the name Torpedo.","wiki_en":"FC Torpedo NN Nizhny Novgorod","wiki_ru":"Торпедо-Виктория"},{"name":"Portugal national football team","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Portugal","1914"],"description":"The national team of a small country with an outsized football tradition won its first major title at the European Championship in 2016. Several of the players who have worn its shirt were named the best footballer in the world in their day.","fact":"Portugal's first big trophy came at the European Championship, beating the hosts France in the final.","wiki_en":"Portugal national football team"},{"name":"Netherlands national football team","emoji":"🧡","meta":["football","Netherlands","1889"],"description":"The team is known as the creator and chief exponent of the Total Football of the 1970s, which changed how the game was understood. The orange of the shirt refers to the country's ruling royal house.","fact":"For all its three World Cup finals, the Netherlands has never been world champion.","wiki_en":"Netherlands national football team"},{"name":"Uruguay national football team","emoji":"💙","meta":["football","Uruguay","1900"],"description":"One of the most successful national teams of football's early history, twice an Olympic champion and the first world champion, at home in 1930. A small country with the richest of football traditions.","fact":"Uruguay both hosted and won the very first football World Cup, in 1930.","wiki_en":"Uruguay national football team"},{"name":"Croatia national football team","emoji":"♟️","meta":["football","Croatia","since the 1990s"],"description":"The team of independent Croatia made its international debut in the early 1990s, soon after the break-up of Yugoslavia. The red and white chequerboard kit has made it one of the most recognisable sides at World Cups.","fact":"In 2018 Croatia reached the first World Cup final in its history.","wiki_en":"Croatia national football team"},{"name":"Belgium national football team","emoji":"😈","meta":["football","Belgium","1895"],"description":"The side is nicknamed the Red Devils for its bright kit and its pressing style of play. Through the 2010s Belgium had one of the strongest generations in its history, holding high places in the FIFA rankings year after year.","fact":"Belgium picked up the nickname Red Devils back at the start of the twentieth century, because of the colour of its shirts.","wiki_en":"Belgium national football team"},{"name":"Mexico national football team","emoji":"⚽","meta":["football","Mexico","1927"],"description":"One of the most successful national teams of North and Central America and a regular presence at World Cups. Mexico has twice hosted the tournament itself, in 1970 and in 1986, staging both finals at the Estadio Azteca.","fact":"Mexico is one of the few countries to have hosted the football World Cup twice.","wiki_en":"Mexico national football team"},{"name":"Sweden national ice hockey team","emoji":"👑","meta":["ice hockey","Sweden","since the 1920s"],"description":"The team is known as Tre Kronor — the Three Crowns — after the symbol on its shirt, taken from the national coat of arms. Sweden is a constant presence, and often a medallist, at the world championships.","fact":"In 2006 the Swedes won both the Olympic hockey tournament and the world championship — two titles in one year.","wiki_en":"Sweden men's national ice hockey team"},{"name":"Finland national ice hockey team","emoji":"🦁","meta":["ice hockey","Finland","since the 1920s"],"description":"Known as the Leijonat — the Lions — the team has become one of the strongest in world hockey despite the country's comparatively small population. Finland regularly fights for medals at world championships and Olympics.","fact":"The Finns won their first Olympic hockey gold only in 2022, at the Beijing Games.","wiki_en":"Finland men's national ice hockey team"},{"name":"Czechia national ice hockey team","emoji":"🏒","meta":["ice hockey","Czechia","1993"],"description":"Formed after the split of Czechoslovakia, the team inherited the rich hockey tradition of the region. In its first years of independence Czechia already had great international success, Olympic gold included.","fact":"At the 1998 Olympics in Nagano the Czech team took gold, beating the tournament's chief favourites.","wiki_en":"Czech Republic men's national ice hockey team"},{"name":"United States national ice hockey team","emoji":"🏒","meta":["ice hockey","USA","since the 1920s"],"description":"The team went into history with the Miracle on Ice — the win of a student squad over the supposedly unbeatable USSR side at the 1980 Olympics. The United States is a fixture at the world's hockey tournaments.","fact":"The 1980 Olympic win over the Soviet team is regarded as one of the great sporting upsets of the twentieth century.","wiki_en":"United States men's national ice hockey team"},{"name":"Spain national basketball team","emoji":"🏀","meta":["basketball","Spain","since the 1930s"],"description":"One of the strongest basketball sides in Europe this century, a repeat champion of both the continent and the world. The Spanish basketball school has sent a long line of players to the NBA, the strongest league on the planet.","fact":"Spain has won the World Cup twice — in Japan in 2006 and in China in 2019.","wiki_en":"Spain men's national basketball team"},{"name":"Serbia national basketball team","emoji":"🏀","meta":["basketball","Serbia","since 2006"],"description":"The team carried on the rich basketball tradition of the former Yugoslavia after Serbia became independent. It is regularly among the strongest at European and world championships and at the Olympic Games.","fact":"The basketball school of the former Yugoslavia, continued by Serbia, is reckoned one of the strongest in European history.","wiki_en":"Serbia men's national basketball team"}];
